After running `revela generate all`, you have a complete static website in your `output/` folder. This guide shows you how to publish it online.

## What Gets Generated?

Revela creates a fully self-contained static website:

```
output/
├── index.html          # Your homepage
├── 404.html            # "Page not found" page (if the theme provides one, Lumina does)
├── sitemap.xml         # Search engine sitemap (if baseUrl configured)
├── galleries/
│   └── vacation/
│       └── index.html  # A gallery page
├── photo/              # One page per photo (Lumina's default photo viewer)
├── images/             # Optimized images: images/<gallery>/<photo>/<width>.<format>
└── _assets/            # Theme CSS, JavaScript and fonts
```

Files from `source/_static/` (for example `CNAME`, `robots.txt` or favicons) are copied to the output root as well.

The output contains only your site: Revela keeps its own bookkeeping in the project's `.revela/` folder, never in `output/`, so you can upload the whole folder as it is.

**No server-side processing needed!** Any web host that serves HTML files will work.

### Building on a Server or in CI

Encoding images is the slow part of a build (hours for a large library with AVIF). Revela only encodes new or changed photos, as long as it finds both the previous `output/` and the project's `.revela/` folder, which records how the existing image files were made. On a build server, keep both between runs (for example as a persistent working directory or a CI cache). A fresh checkout without them builds everything from scratch, which is correct, just slower. If a plugin keeps durable data in `.revela/` (data it cannot recreate), back that folder up as well.

## Sitemap

Revela automatically generates a `sitemap.xml` for search engine indexing. To enable it,
add `baseUrl` to your `project.json`:

```json
{
  "project": {
    "baseUrl": "https://your-domain.com"
  }
}
```

The sitemap includes all pages with `<lastmod>` dates. Without `baseUrl`, the sitemap is
skipped (sitemaps require absolute URLs). You can also set it with `revela config project --url https://your-domain.com`.

> **Before you publish:** run `revela check`. It catches configuration and content problems and reminds you when `baseUrl` is missing.

`baseUrl` also enables `<link rel="canonical">` and absolute Open Graph URLs (`og:url`,
`og:image`) on every page, so shared links get a proper preview image.

## 404 Page

`revela generate pages` (and `generate all`) writes a `404.html` to the output root, rendered
with your theme's layout and in your site language. It is marked `noindex`, is not part of the
navigation, and is excluded from `sitemap.xml`. Because the web server shows it for *any* missing
URL, all of its links and stylesheets are root-absolute (they start with `/`, or with your
`basePath` such as `/photos/`).

Revela only writes the file; your web server has to serve it for missing pages:

| Host | Setup |
|------|-------|
| **GitHub Pages, Netlify, Cloudflare Pages** | Nothing to do, `404.html` is picked up automatically |
| **`revela serve`** (local preview) | Nothing to do, missing files get `404.html` with status 404 |
| **Caddy** | `handle_errors` block, see below |
| **nginx** | `error_page 404 /404.html;` |
| **Apache** | `ErrorDocument 404 /404.html` (e.g. in `.htaccess`) |

Caddy:

```
handle_errors {
    @404 expression {err.status_code} == 404
    handle @404 {
        rewrite * /404.html
        file_server
    }
}
```

With a `basePath`, point the server at that path instead (for example `/photos/404.html`).

**Customize it:** change the wording through the `notfound.*` keys in the theme's
`Locales/<language>.json` (see [Theme Language](../themes/#theme-language)), or extract and edit
the template with `revela theme extract Lumina --file Body/NotFound.revela`. A hand-written
`source/_static/404.html` replaces the generated page entirely. Themes without a
`Body/NotFound.revela` template produce no 404 page.

## Caching

A sensible default for your web server or CDN:

- **HTML** (`*.html` and directory URLs): `Cache-Control: no-cache`. Browsers revalidate, so
  visitors see a new build right away.
- **Images** (`/images/`): a long cache, e.g. `Cache-Control: public, max-age=2592000` (30 days).
  Variant file names stay the same when you replace a photo under the same name, so only go
  up to a year with `immutable` if you never do that.
- **Theme assets** (`/_assets/`): cache for a year, `Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable`.
  Themes link their CSS and JavaScript through `asset_url`, which adds a fingerprint of the file
  content (`main.css?v=3f2a9c1d`). Change the file and the URL changes with it, so browsers fetch
  the new version on the next page view, even home-screen web apps on a phone. Unchanged files keep
  their URL and stay cached. One exception: files a stylesheet loads by itself (for example a font
  in `url(fonts/inter.woff2)`) have no fingerprint. Give such a file a new name when you change it.

Caddy example:

```
@html path *.html */
header @html Cache-Control "no-cache"
@images path /images/*
header @images Cache-Control "public, max-age=2592000"
@assets path /_assets/*
header @assets Cache-Control "public, max-age=31536000, immutable"
```

With a `basePath`, prefix the paths (for example `/photos/_assets/*`).

## Hosting Options

| Option | Cost | Difficulty | Best For |
|--------|------|------------|----------|
| **GitHub Pages** | Free | Easy | Developers, version control |
| **Netlify** | Free tier | Easy | Drag & drop, custom domains |
| **Cloudflare Pages** | Free tier | Easy | Fast global CDN |
| **Traditional Hoster** | Varies | Easy | Existing hosting |
| **Own Server** | Varies | Medium | Full control |

## Option 1: GitHub Pages

This is how [revela.website](https://revela.website) is hosted. Free, fast, and integrates with Git.

### Manual Upload

1. Create a GitHub repository (e.g., `my-portfolio`)
2. Upload the contents of your `output/` folder
3. Go to **Settings → Pages**
4. Select **Branch: main** and **/ (root)**
5. Your site is live at `https://username.github.io/my-portfolio/`

### Automated with GitHub Actions

To build and deploy automatically on every push, commit your project (`project.json`, `site.json`, `source/`, and `themes/` if you have one) to the repository. Then set **Settings → Pages → Source** to **GitHub Actions** and create `.github/workflows/deploy.yml`:

```yaml
name: Deploy Portfolio

on:
  push:
    branches: [main]
  workflow_dispatch:

permissions:
  contents: read
  pages: write
  id-token: write

jobs:
  build:
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    steps:
      - uses: actions/checkout@v4

      - name: Download Revela (Standalone, newest release)
        env:
          GH_TOKEN: ${{ github.token }}
        run: |
          TAG=$(gh release list --repo Spectara/Revela --limit 1 --json tagName --jq '.[0].tagName')
          gh release download "$TAG" --repo Spectara/Revela --pattern "revela-linux-x64-standalone.tar.gz"
          tar -xzf revela-linux-x64-standalone.tar.gz
          chmod +x ./revela

      - name: Generate site
        run: ./revela generate all

      - uses: actions/upload-pages-artifact@v5
        with:
          path: output

  deploy:
    needs: build
    runs-on: ubuntu-latest
    environment:
      name: github-pages
      url: ${{ steps.deployment.outputs.page_url }}
    steps:
      - id: deployment
        uses: actions/deploy-pages@v5
```

The Standalone edition has the theme and all plugins built in, so nothing else needs to be installed. To pin a version for reproducible builds, replace `$TAG` with a release tag such as `v0.0.1-beta.21`.

> **Photos in OneDrive?** Add `./revela source onedrive sync` before `./revela generate all` (see [OneDrive Source](../../plugins/source-onedrive/)).

## Option 2: Netlify (Drag & Drop)

The easiest option if you're not using Git:

1. Go to [netlify.com](https://www.netlify.com/) and sign up
2. Drag your `output/` folder onto the page
3. Done! You get a URL like `random-name.netlify.app`
4. Optionally connect a custom domain

## Option 3: Traditional Web Host (FTP)

If you have existing web hosting (1&1, Strato, HostEurope, etc.):

1. Open your FTP client (FileZilla, WinSCP, etc.)
2. Connect to your host
3. Upload the contents of `output/` to your web root (often `public_html/` or `htdocs/`)
4. Done!

### Using Command Line

```bash
# With rsync (Linux/macOS)
rsync -avz --delete output/ user@yourserver:/var/www/html/

# With lftp
lftp -e "mirror -R output/ /public_html; quit" -u user,password ftp.yourhost.com
```

## Option 4: Cloudflare Pages

Fast global CDN with generous free tier:

1. Push your project to GitHub/GitLab
2. Go to [pages.cloudflare.com](https://pages.cloudflare.com/)
3. Connect your repository
4. Build command (downloads the newest Standalone release, then builds):

   ```bash
   TAG=$(curl -s https://api.github.com/repos/Spectara/Revela/releases | grep -m1 '"tag_name"' | cut -d '"' -f4) && curl -L "https://github.com/Spectara/Revela/releases/download/$TAG/revela-linux-x64-standalone.tar.gz" | tar xz && ./revela generate all
   ```

5. Output directory: `output`

## Custom Domain

Most hosts support custom domains. General steps:

1. Buy a domain (Namecheap, Squarespace Domains, etc.)
2. Add a CNAME record pointing to your host:
   - GitHub Pages: `username.github.io`
   - Netlify: `your-site.netlify.app`
3. Configure the custom domain in your host's settings
4. Wait for DNS propagation (up to 24h)

## Tips

### Base Path for Subdirectories

If your site is hosted at `example.com/photos/` instead of the root:

```json
// project.json
{
  "project": {
    "basePath": "/photos/"
  }
}
```

### Image CDN

For large portfolios, consider hosting images separately on a CDN:

```json
// project.json
{
  "project": {
    "assetsBasePath": "https://cdn.example.com/images/"
  }
}
```

### HTTPS

Always use HTTPS! Most modern hosts provide free SSL certificates:
- GitHub Pages: Automatic
- Netlify: Automatic
- Cloudflare: Automatic
- Traditional: Let's Encrypt (free)

## Real Example: revela.website

This documentation site is built with Revela and deployed to GitHub Pages. See:

- **Source:** [samples/revela-website](https://github.com/Spectara/Revela/tree/main/samples/revela-website)
- **Workflow:** [.github/workflows/deploy-website.yml](https://github.com/Spectara/Revela/blob/main/.github/workflows/deploy-website.yml)
- **Result:** [revela.website](https://revela.website)
